// game.ron — tuning: Vector Gear, camera, physics.
//
// All lengths in METERS (1 Bevy unit = 1 meter, docs/conventions.md).
// The backlog counts in studs; the conversion happens HERE, once, at 0.28 m/stud
// (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-002) — there are no studs in the code.
//
// ⚠️ UNTUNED. These numbers are starting values, not balancing. That is exactly what this
// file is for: changing them costs no rebuild. The P1 gate is a blind test against the
// reference work, and until that is passed nothing here is finished (bible 6.2).
//
// ⚠️ EXCEPTION: whatever comes from assets/data/scale.ron is NOT untuned — it is given by
// the user (2026-08-09) and only mirrored here so the game systems have a number.
// Affected are player.height_m, player.eye_height_m, camera.fov_deg and
// vector.hook_range_m. tests/data.rs falls over the moment one of them drifts away from
// scale.ron. **A direct figure in meters from the user beats any derivation** — including
// the conversion out of the backlog (Q-002).
(
    // 60 Hz fixed simulation. The image interpolates in between; over the network a
    // frame-dependent result is not a comfort problem, it is desync (init.md §6 rule 4).
    simulation_hz: 60.0,
    // Solver substeps per simulation step. avian's own default is 6
    // (avian3d-0.7.0/src/dynamics/solver/schedule.rs:185-191) — and that is measurably too
    // few for this game. 24 is the ONLY measured value that holds both ends at once: the
    // swing loses 4.26 %/s instead of 8.97 %/s at 6, and the worst wall penetration over 18
    // measured cases stays at -0.0043 m of the -0.01 m allowed. It costs 0.72 ms/tick with
    // 20 players in a 401-body city against a 4 ms budget.
    // NOT untuned — this number was measured. Whoever lowers it measures again.
    substeps: 24,
    gravity_m_s2: -20.0,   // harder than 9.81: momentum play, not simulation

    player: (
        // = scale.ron:reference.human_height_m. The user writes "check the capsule exactly!"
        // — so EXACTLY, not roughly: every size comparison in the image hangs on this number.
        height_m: 1.8,
        radius_m: 0.35,
        // P5. ⚠️ UNTUNED. Two ways to lose is the bar (docs/PLAN-GAME.md §1), and one of
        // them is this number reaching zero. 100 against a husk's damage of 34 means
        // **three strikes and you are down** — which is what makes a telegraphed wind-up
        // worth reading instead of tanking. A downed player is a STATE with a timer, not a
        // removed entity (shared::MovementState::Downed).
        health: 100.0,
        run_speed_m_s: 6.0,
        // 6.5 m/s at g = −20 give 6.5²/(2·20) = **1.056 m of apex**. That is enough for
        // neither a door (2.10 m; that would take 9.17 m/s) nor the eaves of the small
        // house (3.00 m). **Without gas you get on top of nothing in this city** — for a
        // Vector Gear game that is right, but it is a decision and not a side effect, and
        // that is why it stands here.
        jump_speed_m_s: 6.5,
        // F-006 air control. ⚠️ UNTUNED. Stood in Rust as `-gravity_m_s2 / 2` until
        // 2026-08-12 (docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-051) — a derivation is still a game value, and §4
        // says a game value lives here. **10.0 is half of -gravity_m_s2, deliberately: WASD
        // alone can never hold you up**, so what keeps a player airborne stays the rope and the
        // gas (docs/NEXT.md §1f, „bis das gas ausgeht"). Two bounds come out of that and both
        // survive the move: strictly **below 20** (= -gravity_m_s2), or gasless hovering is
        // free — tests/data.rs falls over on that one — and well below vector.boost_m_s2: 34,
        // or Shift stops being the strong option (10 is 29 % of it today).
        // ⚠️ Coupled: tests/player.rs::f014_the_input_still_steers_the_carried_momentum asserts
        // > 10° of turn and measures 11.22° — the margin is 1.22°, so anything below ≈10.6
        // takes that test red. That is the guard working, not flake.
        air_accel_m_s2: 10.0,
        air_accel_empty_fraction: 0.5,
        // What is left of it with an empty tank. ⚠️ UNTUNED, and **not a derivation — the
        // user's spec**: „ohne gas kann man immernoch w a d nutzen um etwas movement aufzubauen
        // (aber hälfte ca)" (docs/NEXT.md §1e). So the air control is not gated on gas, it only
        // gets weaker, and an empty tank stops being the dead end behind „seile ohne boost
        // bringen gar nichts". Dimensionless — no unit suffix.
        // 1.65 -> 1.60: the user gives the camera height directly
        // (scale.ron:camera.height_m). The old 1.65 was estimated from the body height;
        // a stated figure beats an estimate.
        eye_height_m: 1.6,
        // Largest distance per substep of the integrator. At max_speed_m_s = 75 a 60 Hz
        // tick covers 1.25 m — without substeps the player drives through every wall, and
        // only sometimes. Has to be strictly smaller than world.min_wall_m, or
        // tests/data.rs falls over. 0.25 m give 5 substeps at top speed.
        max_substep_m: 0.25,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED, and INVENTED — no source, no measurement of its own. The reasoning:
        // on the ground the horizontal velocity is written directly (src/player/locomotion.rs),
        // so friction brakes nothing you want braked; what it WOULD brake is a swinging
        // player who grazes a wall, and a measurement puts that at 75 % of the speed per
        // second at a combined 0.65. The player therefore carries 0.0 and the combine rule
        // `Min`, so that no wall can put its own friction on him.
        // The consequence of `Min`: at exactly 0.0 this knob pins the combined value to 0,
        // whatever the wall carries. Above 0.0 it is a real knob again.
        friction: 0.0,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED, but not invented: 0.0 is avian's own default and means "does not
        // bounce". A player who rebounds off a wall is a bug, not a feature.
        restitution: 0.0,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED and INVENTED. How steep a surface may be and still count as ground.
        // **Measures nothing today:** every body in this world is an axis-aligned cuboid, so
        // every contact normal is exactly ±X, ±Y or ±Z, and every value strictly between 0
        // and 90 gives the identical result. The number becomes real the day a ramp does.
        max_ground_slope_deg: 50.0,
    ),

    vector: (
        // 112.0 -> 90.0. The 112 came out of 400 studs x 0.28 (Q-002). The user gives the
        // anchor range directly as **90 m** (scale.ron:vector.anchor_range_m), and a direct
        // figure in meters beats any derivation. Consequence for Q-002: 90/400 would be
        // 0.225 m/stud — the factor 0.28 still holds for everything the user has said
        // nothing about, but it has become questionable.
        // 90 -> 200 (scale.ron:vector.anchor_range_m, and tests/data.rs falls over if the two
        // ever drift apart). The user, 2026-08-10, after playing it: *"zudem muss die hook
        // range sehr viel laenger sein!"* — see docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-035 for why 200 and not
        // 400.
        hook_range_m: 200.0,
        // 90 -> 160. NOT a taste change: it follows from the range. A hook is a projectile,
        // so at the old 90 m/s a shot to the new maximum range would take **2.22 s** to
        // arrive — longer than most of a swing, and long enough that the anchor you aimed at
        // is not where you are any more. 160 m/s holds the worst-case flight at 1.25 s, which
        // is what a 90 m shot cost before (1.0 s) plus a little. ⚠️ UNTUNED.
        hook_speed_m_s: 160.0,
        // F-001 names „einziehend" as the fourth hook state; game.ron knew only the outward
        // flight so far. Faster than the outward flight, so a missed shot is not punished.
        // ⚠️ Pure starting value, no source (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-016).
        hook_retract_speed_m_s: 120.0,
        reel_speed_m_s: 28.0,        // F-005 Reel-In
        min_rope_m: 3.0,          // any closer would drag the camera into the wall
        // Gauss-Seidel iterations over both rope constraints. With one iteration the second
        // constraint violates the first again; two are enough in the normal case (two roofs,
        // angle over 60 degrees) for under 1 % residual error. The degenerate case — both
        // anchors nearly in one line — does not converge with two either (docs/interface.md).
        rope_iterations: 2,
        // ⚠️⚠️ THERE IS NO `gas_regen_per_s` AND NO `gas_regen_delay_s` IN THIS BLOCK, AND
        // THAT IS A DECISION — NOT SOMETHING SOMEBODY FORGOT. DO NOT ADD ONE.
        //
        // The user, 2026-08-12, closing docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-033: "gas refillt nur im main
        // gebaeude an bestimmten stationen/objekten". Gas comes back at a PLACE YOU GO TO,
        // never on a timer. Between 2026-08-10 and 2026-08-12 this file did carry a 10/s
        // regeneration behind a 0.5 s pause; he was asked which of three shapes he wanted and
        // picked none of them. The whole mechanism was removed with those two keys —
        // `refill_tank`, `arm_pause` and `Gas::regen_delay_left_s` went with it.
        //
        // Why he is right: the bible couples gas to RISK, not to a clock — burning it is loud
        // and a Bellower answers the noise (bible line 159). A tank that quietly fills itself
        // while you hang around on a roof is exactly the timer the bible refuses; a tank you
        // have to fly home to fill turns the whole map into a resource decision. The refuel
        // stations are a WORLD feature and they are queued in docs/NEXT.md §1d.
        //
        // If the tank feels tight before those exist, the knob is gas_tank / gas_boost_per_s
        // below, NOT a rate. `deny_unknown_fields` in src/data/mod.rs means either key put
        // back here crashes the game on load rather than being ignored, and
        // tests/vector_gas.rs::f018_an_idle_tank_never_refills_on_its_own goes red the moment
        // anything in the simulation puts a drop back.

        // 100.0 -> 300.0. The user, after playing it on 2026-08-10: "also gas tank sollte
        // sehr viel mehr haben". ⚠️ UNTUNED. At 18/s this is the whole answer to his other
        // sentence, "der boost haelt nicht lang genug": 16.67 s of continuous boost against
        // the 5.6 s he flew. **The length of a boost is gas_tank / gas_boost_per_s and
        // nothing else** — with no refill anywhere, this number IS the flight time, and it is
        // also the whole mission's supply until the stations exist.
        gas_tank: 300.0,
        gas_boost_per_s: 18.0,
        // For scale, the other consumer: reeling a full rope in from 90 m to the 3 m minimum
        // takes (90-3)/28 = 3.1 s and costs 18.6 gas — 6.2 % of the tank.
        gas_reel_per_s: 6.0,
        // Who pays first when the tank is not enough for both? That is a GAME-VALUE
        // decision and not an `if` in the code (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-017).
        gas_priority: [Boost, ReelIn],
        boost_m_s2: 34.0,
        // How much of the boost DIRECTION comes from the rope instead of from the look
        // direction: 0.0 = pure look direction (the behaviour up to 2026-08-10), 1.0 = straight
        // at the anchor. The blend is a direction only — the STRENGTH stays boost_m_s2 at every
        // value (tests/vector_boost.rs).
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED, and the number the user has to feel rather than read. It exists because
        // he asked for it after playing it on 2026-08-10: „wenn man boostet soll man in
        // richtung seil und mauszeiger fliegen!"
        //
        // Why not 1.0, which is what „in richtung seil" says literally: thrust straight at the
        // anchor is RADIAL, and a taut rope eats a radial pull — it adds no tangential speed
        // and so it kills the swing instead of feeding it. Why not 0.0, which is what we had:
        // the rope then steers nothing at all. 0.5 is the middle where looking at your own hook
        // gives you nearly the whole pull while looking away still steers.
        //
        // What the rope half BUYS, and it is the reason this key exists at all: since B-005 the
        // enforced length ratchets down to the true distance every substep, so flying at your
        // anchor permanently SHORTENS the rope — and a shorter rope is what lifts the bottom of
        // the arc off the ground (measured in B-005: 53.3 m of rope -> 20.2 m moved the arc
        // bottom from 18.3 m underground to 14.8 m above it). Look-only boost cannot reach that
        // loop; this is what turns a dead leash into a swing.
        boost_rope_fraction: 0.5,
        // Velocity clamp from day 1, not retrofitted (bible 6.4, risk of fling exploits)
        //
        // ⚠️ Computed against the city of the size table, and the numbers do not fit
        // together (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-018): pure swinging off an 11.5 m roof gives
        // 17-21 m/s, the windups of the titans stay readable up to 6-20 m/s, and 75 m/s
        // only comes out of a full reel-in along a clear 30 m line of sight. 75 m/s crosses
        // the 400 m graybox in 5.33 s and turns a 7 m street into a curve at 164 g and
        // 1228 degrees/s of yaw rate. The number stays standing anyway: it is a clamp
        // against exploits, not a target speed, and what the factor of x1.5 named by the
        // user refers to is open. It is not changed before there is an answer.
        max_speed_m_s: 75.0,
    ),

    camera: (
        // 90.0 -> 60.0. The user names "FOV ground combat 55-65 degrees" and calls it the
        // "biggest lever". The words GROUND COMBAT are the key: 60 degrees is the BASE on
        // the ground, not the upper limit. A 90-degree image makes every titan small and
        // every meter short — exactly what this game does not want.
        // Window: scale.ron:camera.min_ground_fov_deg/-max_ground_fov_deg, tests/data.rs checks it.
        //
        // ⚠️ The number is read VERTICALLY — src/render/mod.rs puts it into
        // `PerspectiveProjection.fov`, and Bevy documents the field as "the vertical
        // field of view". On 16:9 that is 91.5 degrees horizontal (before: 121.3). Whether
        // the user means his 55-65 degrees vertically or horizontally nobody has decided,
        // and the two readings differ by a factor of 1.7: docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-021.
        fov_deg: 60.0,
        // Field of view at max_speed_m_s. F-017 „Geschwindigkeits-Feedback" will later
        // interpolate between fov_deg and this value — the curve belongs in the code, the
        // two ends belong here.
        // ⚠️ STARTING VALUE, not from the user: 90 degrees is exactly the value that stood
        // here until 2026-08-09. At the top end the image thus stays as it was; only ground
        // combat pulls together. That is the smallest change that satisfies the requirement
        // — not an invented second number. F-017 names „abschaltbar fuer Motion Sickness";
        // whoever wants the effect off sets this value to fov_deg.
        fov_max_speed_deg: 90.0,
        mouse_deg_per_px: 0.08,
        pitch_limit_deg: 89.0,
        smoothing_half_life_s: 0.05,
    ),

    world: (
        // Edge length of one grid cell of the spatial index (T-036a).
        // ⚠️ UNMEASURED. docs/lessons/performance.md says it literally: "§11 says
        // ‚Gitterzellen', not how big. Has to be measured." A 90 m ray at 8 m crosses
        // about 11 cells horizontally and 15 vertically at the wall
        // (docs/QUESTIONS.md **Q-014** — until 2026-08-09 a reference to Q-013 stood here,
        // and Q-013 is the question about the maximum rope length).
        cell_m: 8.0,
        // Half edge length of the grid. Has to cover the map AND a full hook range
        // beyond it.
        // 320.0 -> 300.0: recomputed with the new range. 400/2 + 90 = 290 -> 300
        // (rounded up to a round number). The 320 belonged to 112 m (400/2 + 112 = 312).
        // In HEIGHT the same number covers wall (120 m) + Ashwalker (150 m) = 270 m with
        // 30 m to spare. Whether the grid is three-dimensional at all is decided by Q-014;
        // tests/data.rs pins the 270 down anyway, because a grid smaller than the world
        // would be wrong either way.
        //
        // **300 -> 400 on 2026-08-10**, forced by `hook_range_m` 90 -> 200 (Q-035): the grid
        // has to carry half the map plus one full hook range, and `tests/data.rs` fires the
        // moment it does not — 400/2 + 200 = **400 exactly**, no rounding left over.
        // The cost is real and is written here so nobody has to re-derive it: the grid covers
        // 800 x 800 m instead of 600 x 600 at `cell_m: 8.0`, i.e. **10 000 cells against
        // 5 625 — 1.78x the index memory** for the same city. It buys nothing by itself; it
        // is the price of the longer rope. If the index ever becomes a memory problem, the
        // lever is `cell_m`, not this number, and it is measured in
        // docs/lessons/performance.md before it is touched.
        // **400 -> 600 on 2026-08-12**, to make room for a real district. The guard is
        // `half_extent_m >= longest_map_edge/2 + hook_range_m`, so 600 buys a map of up to
        // **800 x 800 m** at the current 200 m range — the graybox's 400 m cleared it with
        // exactly zero margin and could not have grown by a metre.
        // Cost, stated so nobody re-derives it: the grid covers 1200 x 1200 m instead of
        // 800 x 800 at `cell_m: 8.0`, i.e. **22 500 cells against 10 000 — 2.25x the index
        // memory**. That is the price of a district with a 120 m wall in it, and it buys
        // nothing on its own.
        half_extent_m: 600.0,
        // Whoever occupies more cells lands in the linear large-body list instead of in the
        // grid. The ground (400 x 400 m) occupies 2500 cells at 8 m cells; every horizontal
        // ray would otherwise test it once per visited cell.
        large_body_cells: 64,
        // The thinnest wall there may be. Calibrates max_substep_m.
        // ⚠️ In NO source: models.ron A-080 names a 4-stud grid, that is the module width,
        // not the thickness (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-015).
        min_wall_m: 0.5,
        // Collision margin: the distance at which a body comes to a stop in front of a
        // surface. Without it every contact jitters, and the number lands as 1e-3 in Rust.
        collision_margin_m: 0.01,
    ),
)
